Intel(R) VTune(TM) Profiler Command Line Tool
Copyright (C) 2009 Intel Corporation. All rights reserved.

 Analyze the most time-consuming GPU kernels, characterize GPU utilization
 based on GPU hardware metrics, identify performance issues caused by memory 
 latency or inefficient kernel algorithms, and analyze GPU instruction
 frequency per certain instruction types.

 To modify the analysis type, use the configuration options (knobs) as
 follows:
 -collect gpu-hotspots -knob <knobName>=<knobValue>
 Multiple -knob options are allowed and can be followed by additional collect
 action options, as well as global options, if needed. 


dump-compute-task-binaries

  Enable dumping of compute task binary files. By default, this option is set
  to "true". If in-kernel profiling or viewing compute task source-assembler 
  code is not needed, disabling this option can reduce runtime overhead and
  save disk space.

  Default value: true
  Possible values: true false


gpu-profiling-mode

  Select a profiling mode to either characterize GPU performance issues based
  on GPU hardware metric presets or enable a source analysis to identify
  basic blocks latency due to algorithm inefficiencies, or memory latency due
  to memory access issues.

  Default value: characterization
  Possible values: characterization source-analysis 


characterization-mode

  Select a predefined GPU metric group to characterize code performance on
  Intel Graphics: <ul> <li><b>Overview</b> metrics to analyze sampler,
  general memory and cache accesses;</li> <li><b>Global Memory Accesses</b>
  metrics to track accesses to different types of GPU memory;</li>
  <li><b>Compute Extended</b> to analyze additional metrics;</li>
  <li><b>Video Codec Activity</b> metrics to analyze video decoder
  activity;</li> <li><b>Video Enhancement Activity</b> metrics to analyze
  video processor activity;</li> <li><b>Dynamic Instruction Count</b> to
  analyze the frequency of executing GPU instructions, sorted by instruction 
  type.</li> </ul>

  Default value: overview
  Possible values: overview global-memory-accesses compute-extended video-decode video-enhancement instruction-count 


collect-memory-bandwidth

  Collect the data required to compute memory bandwidth.

  Default value: false
  Possible values: true false


collect-programming-api

  Analyze DPC++, OpenCL, and Intel Media SDK programs running on Intel
  Processor Graphics. This option may affect the performance of your
  application on the CPU side.

  Default value: true
  Possible values: true false


gpu-sampling-interval

  Specify an interval (in milliseconds) between GPU samples.

  Default value: 1
  Possible values: numbers between 0.1 and 10 


stall-reasons-sampling-interval

  Specifies sampling interval (in milliseconds) for stall reasons collection.
  Applicable only for Source Analysis mode Stall reasons.

  Default value: 0.0005
  Possible values: numbers between 0.0001 and 2 


source-analysis

  Select a profiling mode: <ul> <li><b>Basic Blocks Latency</b> to identify
  basic blocks latency due to algorithm inefficiencies;</li> <li><b>Memory
  Latency</b> to explore memory access issues;</li> <li><b>Stall Reasons</b> 
  to explore stall issues;</li> </ul>

  Default value: bb-latency
  Possible values: bb-latency mem-latency stall-sampling 


computing-tasks-of-interest

  Specify comma-separated list of GPU computing task names. Use search
  strings if necessary (* and . are supported). Invocations happen in this
  format: <b>computing_task_name[#start_idx#step#stop_idx] </b>Default value:
  *#1#1#4294967295 where computing_task_name : Name of GPU computing
  task(default value *) start_idx : Number of the first invocation to be
  profiled (default value 1) step_idx : Number of step idx invocation
  (default value 1) stop_idx : Number of the last invocation to be profiled
  (default value 4294967295, UNIT_MAX) 

  Default value: 
  


analyze-power-usage

  Enable energy consumption characterization and overtime tracking, with
  breakdown by devices.

  Default value: false
  Possible values: true false

